"""Text for the M3/M5 manuscript revision; scientific records remain frozen."""

ABSTRACT = """Scientific planning agents must distinguish favorable review scores from permission to release evidence-supported claims. We implemented a controller that combines shared quality thresholds, separate evidence review and mechanically checked quotation receipts. Six synthetic task templates under eight conditions yielded 48 paired component inputs. The original controller released all 36 ineligible candidates; selective authorization withheld all 36 and released 10 of 12 eligible candidates, whereas unconditional withholding released none. In 72 constrained generation runs, selective authorization released correct numerical answers in 16 of 18 clean runs. All initial generated artifacts were already eligible, so these runs do not demonstrate repair. In 96 post hoc supplementary evaluations, selective authorization and ordinary second review each withheld 36/36 ineligible inputs and released 9/12 eligible inputs, with 1.76-fold token use for selective authorization. Removing mechanical checks from unmodified receipts changed 0/48 decisions. A subsequent offline study used 371 paired receipt assessments without new model calls. Mechanical checks rejected all 77 constructed constraint violations from nine previously accepted receipts. However, forcing positive verdicts and removing counterevidence released 12/36 ineligible candidates; substituting genuine but non-supporting quotations released 30/36. These constructed interventions demonstrate enforceable receipt constraints while exposing dependence on semantic judgments. The study supports bounded, inspectable release authorization, without establishing an accuracy advantage over ordinary second review."""

INTRO_END = """The formal comparison shows that C can withhold ineligible candidates while releasing some eligible candidates. It also exposes semantic false rejections and a shared upstream scoring bottleneck. Post hoc language-coverage and locator checks, followed by a contemporaneous ordinary-second-review comparison, narrow the interpretation: a second evidence review matched C's aggregate outcomes, and mechanical checks changed no decisions on the observed unmodified receipts. A further offline receipt-intervention study demonstrates their effect on constructed constraint violations and identifies semantic failures that pass those checks. The contribution is a bounded empirical study of an implemented release interface, paired positive and negative controls, and an explicit account of its trust boundaries."""

RELATED_WORK = """### Relation to evidence evaluation and runtime enforcement

Fine-grained factual assessment and citation evaluation already distinguish source-supported statements from unsupported ones. FActScore decomposes long-form text into atomic facts [10], and ALCE evaluates generated answers and citation quality [11]. RARR researches attribution and revises unsupported content [21], while Chain-of-Verification uses verification questions before producing a final response [22]. CiteCheck studies citation faithfulness with positive and negative examples [23]. Answering with Faithfulness evaluates unified and composed methods for answer generation and faithfulness prediction, using predicted faithfulness to select a fallback response [24]. Accordingly, neither additional verification, claim decomposition nor conditioning downstream behavior on evidence is new in itself.

Runtime enforcement also has direct precedents. AgentDojo measures legitimate task completion alongside injection outcomes [16]. CaMeL separates control and data flows and enforces capability policies [25]. AgentSpec provides event-triggered predicates and enforcement actions, including task-completion events [26]. Progent enforces tool and argument privileges, distinguishing policy narrowing from expansion [27], while Fides constrains information flows using confidentiality and integrity labels [28]. FORGE uses explicit policies, provenance and a reference monitor for agent actions; it also discusses the dependence of guarantees on semantic classifiers [29]. These systems establish relevant precedents for enforcing constraints outside a language model.

Our comparison addresses the release of constrained scientific-planning outputs under specified evidence and verifier-response faults. It does not claim a new general authorization principle or equivalent guarantees to these policy systems. Supplementary Table 5 compares twelve works across the verification object, decision basis, trust boundary, resources and empirical coverage. The comparison is descriptive and source-scoped; it does not rank systems using incomparable benchmarks or treat unverified details as absent."""

NATURAL_ABLATION = """A further diagnostic reused all 48 C receipts. Replacing full mechanical receipt checking with a semantic-label-only rule changed 0/48 decisions. We denote this weaker deterministic rule W below; it is not D and makes no new model call. The result shows that mechanical checks did not affect release decisions on these observed unmodified receipts. Together with the ordinary-second-review comparison, it prevents attributing the measured improvement over A specifically to the receipt machinery. The following intervention study tests a different question: whether specified modifications to the same saved receipts change that conclusion under a faulty-verifier-response boundary."""

M3_RESULTS = """### Offline receipt interventions expose mechanical effects and semantic limits

The offline study used all 48 saved C receipts and evaluated 371 constructed variants with both C and W, yielding 742 rule decisions and no new model calls. Both rules preserved their original decisions for all 144 identity, ordering and quotation-whitespace controls. Identity replay reproduced C's complete saved reports. The original 0/48 decision difference on unmodified receipts therefore remains unchanged.

The primary analysis selected only the nine previously accepted receipts. Eight constraint-violation families contributed nine variants each, and locator mismatch applied to five receipts, for 77 variants in total. C withheld all 77, whereas W released all 77. Each triggered its specified mechanical error. The interventions altered quotations, source or passage identifiers, source-list coverage, unit coverage, support presence or locator matching (Supplementary Table 4). All variants satisfied the saved JSON schema. The unchanged candidates remained eligible, so withholding a corrupted receipt is not a false rejection of the original intact input. These 77 variants share nine parents and do not represent independent tasks.

Boundary diagnostics exposed failures when the verifier's semantic fields were altered (Table 3). Forcing positive verdicts alone caused no ineligible releases by C (0/36). Additionally removing counterevidence caused 12/36 ineligible candidates to be released, covering all T3 and T6 conflict cases. Replacing factual support with genuine registry quotations that established no numerical result caused 30/36 ineligible releases. C withheld all six variants that relabeled completed-work assertions as plans; W released all six. These latter interventions retained the candidate's assertion of completed experiments.

[[TABLE:m3_boundary]]

In one conflict case, both record_a and record_b remained in checked_sources, but only the supporting record survived in the reference lists. In a missing-evidence case, the purported support explicitly stated that no numerical observation was available. Both altered receipts were accepted by C. Their original explanations were deliberately retained and contradicted the forced supported verdicts; the controller checks that an explanation is nonempty, not that it is semantically consistent with the verdict. These are schema-valid corrupted receipts, not naturally occurring coherent model responses. All 371 paired outputs replayed exactly, with zero execution faults. The results demonstrate specified mechanical enforcement, not natural error rates, adaptive-attack resistance or an accuracy advantage over D."""

DISCUSSION_SEPARATION = """This supports a practical distinction between a candidate's review score and the evidence needed for its release. It does not establish that C has greater release accuracy than ordinary second review: the contemporaneous comparison produced the same aggregate counts, and the unmodified-receipt diagnostic changed no decisions. The offline interventions add narrower evidence that deterministic receipt constraints can change authorization when those constraints are violated. They also show that a structurally acceptable receipt can still authorize an unsupported claim. Explicit runtime enforcement and evidence-conditioned decisions have relevant precedents [24-29]; our contribution is the implementation and controlled diagnosis of this release interface, rather than a new general safety principle.

In the contemporaneous comparison, C used 67,118 tokens versus 38,125 for D despite equal aggregate release counts. The observed 1.76-fold token use does not establish a benefit in release accuracy for that additional consumption, nor does the small comparison establish general equivalence. W is a weaker rule applied to C's saved receipts, so its behavior under receipt intervention cannot substitute for a comparison with D under a matched threat model."""

SEMANTIC_EXTENSION = """The receipt interventions expose a related failure boundary. Authentic quotations do not establish that their meaning supports a claim, and a complete checked_sources list does not establish that relevant opposition was reported. In the positive-label interventions, retaining contradictory explanation text did not prevent release once other blocking fields were removed or replaced. Detecting these semantic inconsistencies or omitted opposition would require capabilities beyond the currently implemented mechanical checks. This limitation concerns faulty verifier responses and should not be conflated with the earlier score-override intervention."""

THREAT_LIMITS = """The experiments cover prescribed evidence faults, a privileged review-field intervention, one fixed instruction in untrusted notes and a separate offline verifier-response intervention. They do not cover adaptive adversaries, malicious controller-code changes, compromised signing or storage systems, long-document retrieval, image interpretation, or unrestricted model-generated plans. The receipt interventions were designed after inspecting the code and saved data. Freezing them before evaluation does not make them preregistered or held out, and their correlated variants cannot estimate natural verifier error rates. Source hashes and transcript replay establish consistency of saved execution, not external scientific truth. There were no independent human expert assessments of the synthetic outputs."""

CONCLUSION = """Overall, the study provides bounded evidence that reviewing supplied evidence can prevent failures of a tested score-driven release rule while retaining some legitimate releases. The offline interventions show that explicit receipt constraints can affect authorization, while identifying semantic failures that those constraints permit. The data support separating review scores, evidence judgments and release records, with clearly stated trust assumptions. They do not establish an accuracy advantage over ordinary second review, comprehensive semantic verification or demonstrated repair of erroneous outputs."""

CONTROLLER_MECHANICS = """The controller checked unit identifiers against all nonblank candidate lines and checked_sources against the supplied source identifiers. It verified that source and passage identifiers existed and that quotations of at least 20 characters matched supplied passages exactly or under limited PDF whitespace normalization. It matched figure locators to supporting passages and rejected unrecorded completed-work assertions. For each reported counterevidence reference, it required a matching source quotation and a sufficiently long quotation from the candidate containing a qualification pattern. These checks do not establish that the verifier reported all relevant opposition or that the qualification is semantically adequate. The reason field is required to be nonempty but is not checked for agreement with the verdict. Every candidate required a factual premise and an explicitly untested proposal. Receipts and decisions were bound to the candidate, evidence library, policy and prompt by hashes; these checks do not independently establish semantic entailment."""

M3_METHODS = """### Offline verifier receipt intervention protocol

We conducted a separate post hoc, code-informed intervention study using all 48 C receipts from the supplementary comparison. The experimenter could replace only the verifier's JSON response immediately before C inspected it. Candidate text, artifacts, scores, corpus, release policy and controller code were unchanged. Each assessment used a fresh controller and one receipt substitution, without retries, repair, recovery or adaptive search. This models a faulty or compromised verifier response, rather than the earlier reviewer-score override. The protocol, executable, source bindings and every variant were frozen before evaluation, after the code and original data had already been inspected.

C was loaded from the frozen source snapshot. W was the exact semantic_only function extracted from that snapshot and used in the earlier same-receipt diagnostic. It required at least one factual row and accepted only supported factual rows or not_applicable planned/heading rows. W was not controller D. All candidates already passed the shared quality thresholds. Both rules received the same altered receipt without private environments, condition identifiers or eligibility labels. The independent Decimal oracle assessed unchanged artifacts after authorization.

Three control families covered all 48 receipts. Nine primary mechanical families were applied only to the nine originally accepted receipts, with locator mismatch applicable to five, yielding 77 variants. Three semantic-field boundary families covered all 48 receipts, and completed-work relabeling covered six, yielding 150 boundary variants. Exact transformations and denominators are in Supplementary Table 4. Original explanations were preserved. Every variant was checked against the frozen schema using an implementation restricted to its actual keywords. A local stub supplied each receipt once; a Python audit hook blocked connections, DNS, binding and child-process execution. Its deliberately blocked self-test was recorded separately from unexpected attempts. Full reports and summary statistics were independently replayed. The study made no new model calls."""

M3_STATISTICS = """For receipt interventions, the primary endpoint was C withholding with W releasing an originally accepted, mechanically corrupted receipt. Candidate eligibility and receipt validity were kept separate. We report each boundary family's total releases and releases among independently ineligible candidates using its own denominator. The 371 assessments derive from 48 receipts and six templates; primary variants share nine accepted parents. They are not independent samples or additional live evaluations and are not pooled with original false-release proportions."""

M3_AUDIT = """The offline receipt study bound all 48 original receipts to saved API responses and retained complete altered receipts, changed-field paths, source bindings and 371 paired outputs. Frozen source, variant and result hashes were checked; independent replay reproduced every full C report, W decision and summary. An isolated extracted copy also passed all 371 pairs. No execution faults, unexpected network attempts or new model calls occurred. The deliberate network self-test was blocked before connecting. The included manifests check file consistency and do not constitute external timestamping or preregistration."""

M3_DATA = """The additional offline verifier-receipt dataset includes the frozen protocol, original receipt bindings, 371 variants, paired decisions, eligibility-stratified summaries and executable replay code. It is supplied separately from the original formal and C/D datasets. Each variant's parent and exact changed fields are retained, and both successful checks and boundary failures are included. The integrated review bundle preserves the earlier experiment archives and adds the revised manuscript, Supplementary Information and a version-specific primary-source comparison."""

M3_LEGEND = """Table 3. Boundary interventions on saved C verifier receipts. The first three families each modify the same 48 receipts, comprising 12 eligible and 36 ineligible candidates; the last family covers six ineligible completed-work assertions. Total releases and ineligible releases have different denominators. Explanations are retained unchanged and may contradict the forced verdict. W is the semantic-label-only rule, not D. These constructed, correlated interventions do not estimate natural error rates or revise the original formal results."""
